   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# portlog\n\nKnow what held a local TCP port—even after the process has exited.\n\n“What was on port 3000 an hour ago?” portlog records which process held each port over time, so you can look back.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns the recorder in the background.\n\n### Look up a port\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nSee every process that held port 3000, newest first—even processes that have exited.\n\nLimit the results to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n### Free a port\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nStop the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation. Add `-y` to skip confirmation.\n\n### Stop recording\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## How it works\n\nStart the background recorder, then query the history when you need it. portlog keeps seven days of history in `~/.portlog/`.\n\nCommand-line arguments can hold secrets. portlog never records them.\n\n## Details\n\nWritten in Go. MIT license.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What does portlog do?\n(b) What do you run first after installing it?\n(c) Can it show a process that already exited?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (c) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
